<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Willy Brandt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1913">1913</a>–<a href="/years/1992">1992</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Herbert Ernst Karl Frahm</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Willy Brandt, born Herbert Ernst Karl Frahm in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, was a German politician who led the Social <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> (SPD) from 1964 to 1987 and was chancellor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> from 1969 to 1974. He fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> under the Nazi regime, taking the name Willy Brandt as a pseudonym, and first won fame as governing mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-berlin">West Berlin</a>. As chancellor he launched <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostpolitik">Ostpolitik</a> to improve relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, and in 1970 knelt in silence at a memorial to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> Ghetto Uprising; he received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1971. He resigned in 1974 after his aide Günter Guillaume was exposed as an agent of the Stasi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/willy-brandt.jpg" alt="Willy Brandt" width="330" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Engelbert Reineke · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_B_145_Bild-F057884-0009,_Willy_Brandt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>West Germany joined NATO in 1955, faced the Berlin Wall from 1961 and eased tensions with the East through <b>Willy Brandt</b>'s Ostpolitik in the late 1960s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-germany">Federal Republic of Germany · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Meierstraße. Died in Unkel, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.6,7.22&amp;year=1964" aria-label="Unkel on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4715 407 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Willy Brandt"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5165 677h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5165 677h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=50.6,7.22&amp;year=1964">What was here in 1964? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
